__registerContent("quickrecall/history/freedom-journey", {"notes":{"qr-freedom-journey":{"era":"Quick Recall — Modern History","significance":["The chronological sequence of freedom-struggle events is one of the highest-frequency Prelims areas and a common 'arrange in order' question","Year-event pairing (e.g. 1906 Muslim League, 1916 Lucknow Pact, 1930 Dandi March) is repeatedly tested","The timeline anchors Mains answers on phases of the national movement and the interplay of moderates, extremists and Gandhian mass politics"],"features":[{"key":"Early Milestones (1857–1911)","main":"1857 — First War of Independence (revolt against British rule)\n1885 — Indian National Congress formed (platform for political representation)\n1905 — Partition of Bengal (divide-and-rule by Curzon)\n1906 — All India Muslim League formed\n1907 — Surat Split (Moderates and Extremists divide)\n1911 — Capital shifted from Calcutta to Delhi","note":"1905 Partition of Bengal triggered the Swadeshi Movement."},{"key":"War Years & Early Gandhi (1914–1919)","main":"1914–18 — World War I (India's resources used in the war)\n1916 — Lucknow Pact (Congress–Muslim League unity)\n1917 — Champaran Satyagraha (Gandhi's first Satyagraha in India)\n1919 — Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (13 April, Amritsar)","note":"1919 also saw the Rowlatt Act and the Government of India Act, 1919 (Montagu-Chelmsford reforms)."},{"key":"Mass Movements (1920–1930)","main":"1920 — Non-Cooperation Movement (nationwide boycott of British institutions)\n1922 — Chauri Chaura incident (movement withdrawn after violence)\n1927 — Simon Commission opposition ('Simon Go Back')\n1929 — Lahore Congress Session (Purna Swaraj / complete independence resolution)\n1930 — Dandi March / Salt Satyagraha (Gandhi's march against the salt tax)","note":"26 January 1930 was first observed as Purna Swaraj (Independence) Day."},{"key":"Towards Freedom (1931–1947)","main":"1931 — Gandhi–Irwin Pact; Second Round Table Conference (Gandhi represents Congress in London)\n1932 — Communal Award & Poona Pact (Gandhi–Ambedkar)\n1935 — Government of India Act (provincial autonomy)\n1937 — Provincial elections (Congress forms ministries)\n1942 — Quit India Movement ('Do or Die')\n1943 — Azad Hind Fauj & Provisional Government (Subhas Chandra Bose)\n1945 — INA Trials; 1946 — Royal Indian Navy Mutiny\n1947 — 15 August: Independence","note":"The Poona Pact (1932) replaced separate electorates for Depressed Classes with reserved seats."}],"traps":[{"label":"Formation years","lines":["INC formed 1885; Muslim League formed 1906 — do not swap.","Surat Split: 1907 (Moderates vs Extremists).","Capital shifted Calcutta → Delhi: 1911."]},{"label":"Movement start/withdrawal","lines":["Non-Cooperation started 1920; withdrawn 1922 after Chauri Chaura.","Purna Swaraj resolution: Lahore Session, 1929.","Dandi March / Salt Satyagraha: 1930."]},{"label":"Champaran vs later Satyagrahas","lines":["Champaran (1917) was Gandhi's FIRST Satyagraha in India.","Quit India ('Do or Die'): 1942."]}],"mainsAngle":"India's freedom struggle evolved from the constitutional moderation of the early Congress, through the assertive extremism after the Bengal Partition, to Gandhian mass mobilisation — reflecting a widening social base and a maturing national consciousness that ultimately made colonial rule untenable.","mainsTheme":"Phases of the national movement — moderates, extremists and Gandhian mass politics","recall":["1857 First War of Independence; 1885 INC formed","1905 Partition of Bengal; 1906 Muslim League; 1907 Surat Split","1916 Lucknow Pact; 1917 Champaran; 1919 Jallianwala Bagh","1920 Non-Cooperation; 1922 Chauri Chaura; 1929 Purna Swaraj (Lahore)","1930 Dandi March; 1931 Gandhi-Irwin Pact; 1932 Poona Pact","1935 GoI Act; 1942 Quit India; 1947 Independence (15 August)"]}},"quizzes":{"qr-freedom-journey":{"title":"India's Freedom Journey (1857–1947)","questions":[{"q":"The Indian National Congress was formed in which year?","options":["1857","1885","1906","1911"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Indian National Congress was formed in 1885 as a platform for political representation."},{"q":"The All India Muslim League was established in:","options":["1905","1906","1907","1916"],"correct":1,"explain":"The All India Muslim League was established in 1906 to represent Muslim interests."},{"q":["Arrange the following events in chronological order:","1. Surat Split","2. Partition of Bengal","3. Formation of the Muslim League","Select the correct order (earliest first):"],"options":["2 → 3 → 1","1 → 2 → 3","3 → 2 → 1","2 → 1 → 3"],"correct":0,"explain":"Partition of Bengal (1905) → Muslim League (1906) → Surat Split (1907)."},{"q":"Gandhi's first Satyagraha in India (1917) was launched at:","options":["Kheda","Champaran","Ahmedabad","Bardoli"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Champaran Satyagraha (1917), on behalf of indigo cultivators in Bihar, was Gandhi's first Satyagraha in India."},{"q":"The Jallianwala Bagh massacre took place in which year?","options":["1917","1919","1920","1922"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Jallianwala Bagh massacre occurred on 13 April 1919 in Amritsar."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The Non-Cooperation Movement began in 1920.","II. It was withdrawn in 1922 following the Chauri Chaura incident.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — Non-Cooperation began in 1920 and was withdrawn in 1922 after the violence at Chauri Chaura."},{"q":"The Purna Swaraj (Complete Independence) resolution was passed at which Congress session?","options":["Surat Session (1907)","Lucknow Session (1916)","Lahore Session (1929)","Karachi Session (1931)"],"correct":2,"explain":"The Purna Swaraj resolution was adopted at the Lahore Congress Session of 1929."},{"q":"The Dandi March (Salt Satyagraha) was launched in:","options":["1928","1930","1932","1942"],"correct":1,"explain":"Gandhi launched the Dandi March / Salt Satyagraha in 1930 against the salt tax."},{"q":["Match the year with the event:","I. 1931 — a) Government of India Act (provincial autonomy)","II. 1935 — b) Gandhi–Irwin Pact","III. 1942 — c) Quit India Movement","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I-b, II-a, III-c","I-a, II-b, III-c","I-c, II-a, III-b","I-b, II-c, III-a"],"correct":0,"explain":"1931 — Gandhi–Irwin Pact (b); 1935 — Government of India Act (a); 1942 — Quit India Movement (c)."},{"q":"The Poona Pact (1932) was signed between Gandhi and:","options":["Jawaharlal Nehru","B. R. Ambedkar","Muhammad Ali Jinnah","Subhas Chandra Bose"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Poona Pact (1932) between Gandhi and Ambedkar replaced separate electorates for the Depressed Classes with reserved seats in a joint electorate."},{"q":"The slogan 'Do or Die' is associated with which movement?","options":["Non-Cooperation Movement","Civil Disobedience Movement","Quit India Movement","Swadeshi Movement"],"correct":2,"explain":"Gandhi gave the 'Do or Die' call during the Quit India Movement of 1942."},{"q":"The capital of British India was shifted from Calcutta to Delhi in:","options":["1905","1911","1919","1931"],"correct":1,"explain":"The capital was shifted from Calcutta to Delhi in 1911."},{"q":"The Azad Hind Fauj (Indian National Army) and the Provisional Government were associated with:","options":["Bhagat Singh","Subhas Chandra Bose","Bal Gangadhar Tilak","Lala Lajpat Rai"],"correct":1,"explain":"Subhas Chandra Bose led the Azad Hind Fauj and set up the Provisional Government of Free India (1943)."},{"q":"The Lucknow Pact (1916) is significant because it marked:","options":["The split between Moderates and Extremists","Congress–Muslim League unity","The launch of the Non-Cooperation Movement","The partition of Bengal"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Lucknow Pact (1916) brought Congress and the Muslim League together, and reunited the Moderates and Extremists within the Congress."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the Partition of Bengal (1905):","I. It was carried out under Lord Curzon.","II. It triggered the Swadeshi Movement.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — the 1905 Partition of Bengal under Curzon triggered the Swadeshi and Boycott movements."},{"q":"'Simon Go Back' was the slogan raised against the Simon Commission in:","options":["1919","1927","1930","1935"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Simon Commission arrived in 1928 and faced nationwide protests ('Simon Go Back'); its appointment (all-White membership) was announced in 1927."},{"q":"India attained independence on:","options":["26 January 1947","15 August 1947","26 November 1949","15 August 1950"],"correct":1,"explain":"India became independent on 15 August 1947."},{"q":"The Government of India Act, 1935 is best known for introducing:","options":["Separate electorates for the first time","Provincial autonomy","Dyarchy at the provincial level","The office of Governor-General"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Government of India Act, 1935 introduced provincial autonomy (replacing provincial dyarchy of 1919) and provided for an all-India federation."},{"q":["Consider the following pairs (Year — Event):","I. 1937 — Provincial elections; Congress forms ministries","II. 1945 — INA Trials","III. 1946 — Royal Indian Navy Mutiny","How many of the above pairs are correctly matched?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":2,"explain":"All three are correctly matched — 1937 provincial elections, 1945 INA trials, and the 1946 Royal Indian Navy Mutiny."},{"q":"The First War of Independence (the Revolt) against British rule occurred in:","options":["1757","1857","1885","1905"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Revolt of 1857 is regarded as the First War of Independence against British rule."}]}}});
